=========================================================
VARIADIC TEMPLATES (C++11/14/17)
Interview Notes
=========================================================

1. What are Variadic Templates?
================================

Variadic Templates allow a function or class template to accept **any number
of template arguments** (zero or more).

Introduced in **C++11**, they eliminate the need to write multiple overloaded
functions for different numbers of arguments.

---------------------------------------------------------

Without Variadic Templates

void print();
void print(int);
void print(int, int);
void print(int, int, int);
void print(int, int, int, int);
...

Problem
-------

• Too many overloads
• Code duplication
• Difficult to maintain

---------------------------------------------------------

With Variadic Templates

template<typename... Args>
void print(Args... args);

One function handles any number of arguments.

=========================================================
2. Syntax
==========

Template Parameter Pack

template<typename... Args>

Function Parameter Pack

Args... args

Example

template<typename... Args>
void print(Args... args)
{
}

Args...

represents zero or more template types.

=========================================================
3. Parameter Pack
=================

A parameter pack is a collection of template arguments.

Example

template<typename... Args>
void func(Args... args)
{
}

Call

func();

Args = {}

----------------------------

func(10);

Args = {int}

----------------------------

func(10, 3.5);

Args = {int, double}

----------------------------

func(10, 3.5, "Hello");

Args = {int, double, const char*}

=========================================================
4. Recursive Variadic Template
==============================

The most common implementation in C++11 uses recursion.

Base Case

void print()
{
    cout << endl;
}

Recursive Function

template<typename T, typename... Args>
void print(T first, Args... rest)
{
    cout << first << " ";

    print(rest...);
}

Usage

print(1);

print(1, 2);

print(1, 2.5, "Hello", 'A');

Output

1

1 2

1 2.5 Hello A

---------------------------------------------------------

How It Works

print(1,2,3)

↓

print(1)

↓

print(2,3)

↓

print(2)

↓

print(3)

↓

print()

(Base Case)

=========================================================
5. sizeof...
=============

sizeof...(Args)

Returns the number of template arguments.

Example

template<typename... Args>
void count(Args... args)
{
    cout << sizeof...(Args);
}

Usage

count(1);

Output

1

----------------------------

count(1,2,3);

Output

3

----------------------------

count(1,2,3,4,5);

Output

5

=========================================================
6. Fold Expressions (C++17)
===========================

Before C++17, recursion was required.

C++17 introduced Fold Expressions, making parameter pack operations much
simpler.

Example

template<typename... Args>
auto sum(Args... args)
{
    return (... + args);
}

Usage

cout << sum(1,2,3,4);

Output

10

---------------------------------------------------------

Right Fold

(args + ...)

---------------------------------------------------------

Left Fold

(... + args)

Most interview examples use a left fold.

=========================================================
7. Variadic Template Class
==========================

Class templates can also use parameter packs.

Example

template<typename... Types>
class Tuple
{
};

Usage

Tuple<int>;

Tuple<int, double>;

Tuple<int, double, string>;

=========================================================
8. Perfect Forwarding (C++11)
=============================

Variadic templates are commonly used with perfect forwarding.

Example

template<typename... Args>
void create(Args&&... args)
{
    // Forward arguments
}

Used by STL functions such as

make_unique()

make_shared()

emplace_back()

